oved my last  box in and  i thought that was kind of a curious  statement right like  so how can one side of this apartment  building this apartment complex  be more secure than another right  um so i decided to check it out to see  if the statement was true like  and how would i be able to go about  obtaining that data  so the city of houston has  a department police beat crime  statistics website that has monthly  analyses of  every single police beat district within  our unit  and not not aggregated in any manner so  you have 92 police beats  you have 69 months in total for each  police beat and you can  go in and sort of download each csv file  and  individually they also sponsor  a rhodes shapefiles as part of their  enterprise gis system  via the houston open data portal so i  downloaded  like i said manually all of the csvs for  my police beat  and then also the city roads gis file  i was only going to do it for a short  segment so it made sense to do that  rather than writing an extended script  and doing the analysis there's 7445  crime entries for my particular  apartment area  right which averages out to three to  four per day so obviously pretty  kind of dangerous and then each  each crime has a lot of attribute data  associated with it  so a date a time from 0 to 23  an offense type the beat number the  premise code which is kind of like the  building  um description so apartment complex  um grocery store things like that the  block range  the street name and the number of  offenses for each crime  and that's just kind of an overview of  what the premise code entails  so you can check to see if it happened  at an apartment complex  or a grocery store or bar or something  like that  so i noticed as i was doing this initial  analysis there were a lot of  problems with the data i wasn't sure if  the date  referred to when the complaint was filed  or if when it occurred same for the hour  there are a couple of months missing  from the data set that i had to ask the  police for  sort of by name some previous month's  date was intermixed  unknowns and then also there was a  change in the tabular format  some columns moved around but they never  really changed their names  so that was the initial problem  and those are the the kinds of crimes  committed  for my police beat i and then  i also thought about some additional  questions right so when are when are  these crimes most likely did that happen  are they when i'm at home or are they at  night like  or should i be walking around my  neighborhood  um and most seem to occur between the  hours of four and six  and the least between the hours of three  and six am  and i'm not sure there's such a spike in  the afternoon it might be because so  many people are heading home from work  and the number of blocks  for the police beat when i checked where  the crimes were most likely to occur it  turned out  that three of the most dangerous blocks  in houston  are located right by my apartment  complex and that's actually  the three red bars that you see there  were actually the way that i would walk  to work in the morning from 5 to 7 a.m  so 20.6 of crimes and the 10h 40 police  beat  are committed in those three blocks  really close to my apartment complex so  that was kind of scary  right and checking to see if the guard  was actually right  in terms of which sides of my building  were more secure  he was the the sides closest  to those three blocks were where more  auto thefts occurred  and the sides further away were at least  a little bit less dangerous  so a lot of it happens a lot of the  crime happened to occur along the metro  rail as well so like i said those are  the most  dangerous blocks the ones marked in red  and  greyhound station has a high crime rate  as well so  which would be the most safe route for  me to walk to work  like i i was really kind of curious  safety is a big component of everything  we do in the oil industry  so there's a lot of extra effort  